\description{
Produces a formatted attrition table from a \code{cf_flow} object. Three output
backends are supported: \code{"flextable"} (best for Word), \code{"gt"} (best for
HTML and LaTeX), and \code{"huxtable"}. The underlying data is built by
\code{\link[=as_attrition_tibble]{as_attrition_tibble()}}, which you can call directly for custom formatting.
}
\section{Output formats}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{"flextable"}} -- uses the \pkg{flextable} and \pkg{officer} packages.
Renders natively to Word (\code{.docx}) via \code{officer::read_docx()}, to PDF
via \code{flextable::save_as_image()}, and to HTML. Best choice when Word is
the primary target.
\item \strong{\code{"gt"}} -- uses the \pkg{gt} package. Renders to HTML
(\code{gt::gtsave(..., "table.html")}), LaTeX
(\code{gt::as_latex()}), and Word (via \code{gt::gtsave(..., "table.docx")}
requires the \pkg{webshot2} package). Best choice when LaTeX or HTML
is the primary target.
\item \strong{\code{"huxtable"}} -- uses the \pkg{huxtable} package. Supports Word, LaTeX,
and HTML output.
}
}

\section{Grouping (\code{group_x} / \code{group_y})}{

Supplying \code{group_x} and/or \code{group_y} repeats the whole attrition table
across a grid: \code{group_x} values become repeated column blocks (N /
Removed / \% removed, headed by a spanning column group), and \code{group_y}
values become repeated row blocks (each preceded by a bold group-heading
row), so the criterion rows appear once but attrition counts are shown
per group combination. This is useful, for example, for showing a
stepped-wedge trial's attrition by site (\code{group_x}) and period
(\code{group_y}). \code{branch_by} cannot be combined with grouping.
}

\section{Shading}{

Cells can be shaded to highlight high-attrition rows/cells:
\itemize{
\item \code{shade = "pct_removed"} or \code{"n_removed"} applies an automatic colour
gradient (\code{shade_palette}, default white to red) scaled across the
values present in step/category rows.
\item \code{shade_fn} gives full control: supply a function that takes the
attrition tibble (as returned by \code{\link[=as_attrition_tibble]{as_attrition_tibble()}}, including
\code{group_x}/\code{group_y} columns when grouped) and returns a character
vector of colours (or \code{NA}) the same length as the tibble, one colour
per row. \code{shade_fn} takes precedence over \code{shade} if both are supplied.
}
}

\section{Hierarchy levels (\code{levels})}{

Supplying \code{levels} repeats the N / Removed / \% removed columns once per
hierarchy level, so a single criterion row reports its effect at every
level side by side -- the reporting form required by the CONSORT cluster
extension (Campbell, Elbourne & Altman, BMJ 2004;328:702-8) and the
within-person extension, where losses at each stage are counted at both
the unit of analysis and the unit of recruitment. For example, with a
cluster-randomised trial where participants nest inside clusters:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{h <- cf_hierarchy(participant = "participant_id", cluster = "cluster_id")
flow <- apply_criteria(dat, crit, hierarchy = h)
as_attrition_table(
  flow,
  levels       = c("participant", "cluster"),
  level_labels = c(participant = "Participants", cluster = "Clusters")
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

produces one row per criterion with a column block for participants and a
column block for clusters, e.g. \emph{"Cluster too small -- 42 participants
removed, 3 clusters removed"}. Shading (\code{shade}/\code{shade_fn}) is computed
\emph{per level} -- each level's colour gradient is scaled across that level's
own values, not across all levels combined. \code{levels} cannot be combined
with \code{group_x}/\code{group_y}/\code{branch_by}/\code{count_by}.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
dat  <- mock_cohortflow(n_participants = 200, seed = 1)
crit <- cf_criteria() |>
  include(~ !is.na(age),     label = "Age recorded",    category = "Valid age") |>
  include(~ age >= 18,       label = "Adults only",      category = "Valid age") |>
  include(~ eligible_screen, label = "Passed screening") |>
  include(~ !is.na(consent_date), label = "Consent recorded", category = "Consent") |>
  exclude(~ withdrew,        label = "Withdrew consent", category = "Consent")
flow <- apply_criteria(dat, crit)

as_attrition_table(flow)                       # flextable (Word-ready)
as_attrition_table(flow, backend = "gt")       # gt (LaTeX/HTML-ready)
as_attrition_table(flow, backend = "huxtable") # huxtable

# Shade cells by percentage removed
as_attrition_table(flow, shade = "pct_removed")

# Grid: sites as columns, periods as rows (stepped-wedge design)
sw   <- mock_stepped_wedge(n_participants = 400, n_periods = 4, seed = 1)
flow_sw <- apply_criteria(sw, crit, id = "event_id")
as_attrition_table(flow_sw, group_x = "site_id", group_y = "period")

# Hierarchy levels: cluster CONSORT reporting
cl <- mock_cluster_rct(n_clusters = 20, seed = 1)
h  <- cf_hierarchy(participant = "participant_id", cluster = "cluster_id")
flow_cl <- apply_criteria(cl, crit, hierarchy = h)
as_attrition_table(flow_cl, levels = c("participant", "cluster"))

# Save to Word
ft <- as_attrition_table(flow)
flextable::save_as_docx(ft, path = "attrition.docx")
}
}
